; j1850_vpw.loom
; An SAE J1850 VPW node (10.4 kbit/s, the GM/Chrysler OBD-II bus) at logic
; level, on a VPW transceiver's TX and RX pins: it transmits the frames the
; host gives it (SOF, header and data, the CRC-8 it computes, EOD) with
; J1850's bitwise arbitration (a loser stops driving and retries once the
; bus has been idle again), receives every frame on the bus with the CRC
; check, answers a frame addressed to it with a one-byte in-frame response
; (IFR) if the host enabled that, and collects the IFR bytes another node
; sends after its own frames.
;
; Written on 2026-11-07, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035): J1850 is in no design
; document. Hardware used: the timed waits and NOW, the per-thread output
; group (OUTGRP and OUT, built for parallel buses), so one copy of the code
; drives a different TX pin in each thread, the FIFOs, LD and ST (built for
; device emulation, the 24C02) for the frame buffers, and the bit engine's
; CRC unit (built for USB's CRC5/CRC16 and CAN's CRC15), here fed with SHO
; to a pin index that is not writable, so it computes J1850's CRC-8
; without touching a pin. No hardware knows VPW: the symbol coding by
; pulse length and level, the symbol timing windows, the arbitration by
; watching every bus edge against the node's own schedule, the
; synchronisation of every symbol to the last bus edge, EOD and EOF, the
; normalization bit and the IFR are firmware.
;
; Weaker than a dedicated J1850 controller (Motorola's BDLC) in four ways:
; a node that loses arbitration stops listening until the bus is idle and
; does not receive the winner's frame (the BDLC does); a node with a frame
; waiting to go ignores the frames other nodes send meanwhile, unless it
; joins one of them at its SOF; it is blind for the first 32 us of every
; symbol (a symbol shorter than 34 us is invalid, and one that ends inside
; those 32 us is reported as a symbol error, but a glitch that ends and
; comes back inside them is not seen; the BDLC has a digital noise filter);
; and its receive windows are the specification's to one tick, 2 us, at
; either end (the measurement is in ticks). The PUSH of a report waits for
; room in OUTQ: a host that leaves OUTQ full holds the node between frames.
;
; Pins: TX = OUT0 in thread 0 and OUT1 in thread 2 (each node's
; transceiver transmit input: 1 drives the bus active, through OUT with
; OUTGRP = OUT<TID/2>), RX = IN0 (the receive output: the bus level, 1
; active). The bus is a wired OR: active (high, about 7 V on the real
; wire) dominates passive (pulled low). A second node on the same chip is
; thread 2, whose reset vector (0x100) jumps to this code: each node has
; its own TX pin, and the board joins them into one bus, as two
; transceivers on one wire would (two threads never share an output,
; SEMANTICS 3, 6.3). Both nodes read the bus on the one input IN0: WAITP
; and JP take the pin as an immediate, and two copies of the program, one
; per pin pair, do not fit the 512 words (2 x 290 words and the buffers),
; so the shared code reads one RX pin. An input cannot contend, so this is
; the board wiring both transceivers' RX outputs, or one of them, to IN0.
; Edges are written with OUT in the slot after the deadline (a pin that
; is not an immediate cannot use the deadline latch, SETP ... D), so each
; lands 1 to 2 slots after its tick: 4 to 11 clocks late, the same for
; every edge to within the 0..3 clock slot grid. A symbol that follows a
; bus edge is timed from the tick at which the node saw that edge, so it
; can be one tick longer when the edge and its sighting straddle a tick:
; at 50 MHz (100 clocks a tick, the sighting about 15 clocks after the
; edge) that is rare; at the tests' 16-clock tick the bus showed short
; symbols of 63.5 to 66.5 us and long ones of 127.5 to 130.5 us, against
; AN1731's 49..79 and 112..145.
;
; Sources. SAE J1850 itself (not free) was not read; every figure below is
; secondary. Read on 2026-11-07: Motorola/Freescale application note AN1731,
; "VPW J1850 Multiplexing and Motorola's Byte Data Link Controller", Table 3
; (VPW pulse width times, p. 7), Figure 2 (logic 0 and 1, p. 6), Figure 4
; and the text of pp. 8-9 (message format, arbitration, the CRC
; polynomial and the receiver's residue $C4, at most 12 data bytes). From
; search-result extracts only, the pages not opened (UNCHECKED): the
; normalization bit, short (64 us) for an IFR without CRC and long (128 us)
; with one (Intel 8XC196Lx user manual, figure 86; ST92F124 data sheet,
; NBSYMS = 0), and the header's K bit (bit 3 of the first header byte, 0 =
; IFR required), from memory (SAE J2178), UNCHECKED.
;   symbol      tx nominal (AN1731 tx min..max)   rx window (AN1731)
;   short       64 us (49..79)                    > 34 and <= 96 us
;   long        128 us (112..145)                 > 96 and <= 163 us
;   SOF, EOD    200 us (182..218)                 > 163 and <= 239 us
;   EOF         280 us (>= 261)                   > 239 us
;   IFS         300 us (>= 280)                   > 280 us
;   a 0 is a short passive or a long active symbol, a 1 a long passive or a
;   short active one; SOF is active, so the first bit is passive and the
;   levels alternate; bytes go most significant bit first; active wins,
;   so a 0 beats a 1 and the lower header wins; the CRC is
;   x^8+x^4+x^3+x^2+1 over header and data, register preset to all ones,
;   sent inverted; a receiver that runs the whole frame, CRC included,
;   through the register finds $C4. After EOD a responder sends the NB
;   (active) and its IFR byte(s); EOF ends the frame.
;
; Timing, in ticks of 2 us (TICK_INT 100 at 50 MHz; the tests run 16
; clocks a tick with every bus time scaled with it). Every symbol is timed
; from the bus edge that began it: the node re-anchors TD at each edge it
; did not make itself (SETD 0 right after the WAITP that saw it) and keeps
; its own schedule for the edges it makes, so two nodes stay in step
; however their tick grids lie. Transmit: short 32 ticks, long 64, SOF
; 100, NB 32 starting 100 ticks after the last fall; IFS 150 ticks of idle
; bus before a SOF; a SOF that rises after 120 ticks of idle bus (an EOF)
; but before our IFS has run is joined at once. Arbitration: a passive
; symbol that the bus ends 8 or more ticks before our own end means a
; shorter symbol won; an active symbol the bus holds 16 ticks past our
; fall means a longer one won; either way the node has let go of the bus
; and retries after the next 150 ticks of idle bus. Receive: an edge is
; seen by a timed WAITP, at most one slot late, and a symbol's length is
; the difference of two NOW readings, so +-1 tick: under 16 ticks invalid
; (seen at the 16-tick check), up to 48 short, 49 to 81 long, 82 to 119
; SOF or EOD, 120 or more EOF (passive) or a break (active).
;
; Host words (INQ). Once, before anything else:
;   word 1   15:8 this node's address, the IFR byte it answers with; 0 =
;            never answer (bits 7:0 must be 0)
; Then any number of frames to send:
;   word A   n, the header and data bytes, 1 to 15 (the CRC is added)
;   n words  one byte each, in 7:0, first byte first
; Reports (OUTQ), one for each frame sent and each frame received:
;   word 1   status: 15 F_RX (1 a received frame, 0 our own); 12:8 a
;            received frame's byte count up to EOD, CRC included, or for
;            our own frame the arbitrations lost before it went (mod 32);
;            0 CRC error, 1 symbol error (a symbol outside every window, a
;            break, EOD inside a byte), 2 overflow (over 31 bytes), 3 an
;            IFR followed (NB seen), 4 we answered with our IFR, 5 our IFR
;            lost arbitration to another responder's
;   word 2   m, the bytes that follow
;   m words  one byte each in 7:0: a received frame's bytes, CRC included,
;            then the IFR bytes; after our own frame, the IFR bytes only
; Each report word is a PUSH that waits for room: a host that leaves OUTQ
; full holds the node between frames.
;
; Proved by the deadline checker (.tick 16, the fastest tick it holds for):
; every edge is followed, within 16 ticks, by the WAITD 16 that opens the
; next symbol's watch; the work after an edge (decoding the bit, storing a
; byte, eight SHO through the CRC unit) runs in that interval, and so does
; the transmit side's choice of the next symbol. The checker's RET goes to
; every return site in the thread, so a subroutine called both from the
; timed paths and from a loop around a POP or a PUSH would make every
; pair unbounded: the receive edge handling is inline, the SHO are unrolled
; and the host frame's CRC loop has its own copy of them.
;
; Memory: 290 words: 0x000 to 0x0FF and 0x101 to 0x124 for the code, all
; in thread 0's section, and at 0x100 thread 2's reset vector, JMP start.
; Node data: 0x180 (thread 0) and 0x1C0 (thread 2), 32 words each: the
; configuration word, then 31 bytes, a byte a word. Threads 1 and 3 must
; not run.
;
; Registers: r0 the byte (sending: shifting out of 15; receiving: built in
; 7:0), r1 its bits to go, r2 a symbol's length or TD - NOW at an edge,
; r3 the buffer pointer, r4 the frame's last byte (its CRC), r5 the level
; of the symbol being sent, r6 the report's status word, r7 scratch and
; the .csr scratch.
;
; Readings taken on the open spec questions (2026-11-07): SAE J1850 was not
; read; timing from NXP AN1731 (secondary).
;   Two nodes on one chip share one program and read the bus on the one
;   input IN0, each driving its own TX pin. The bus is one wire, so one
;   input for both is how it would be wired; COMMON's rule is about two
;   threads writing one output, which this does not do.
;   Edges are ordinary writes 4 to 11 clocks after their tick (the shared
;   program selects its output group by thread, so SETP ... D on a fixed
;   pin is not available); every symbol stays inside AN1731's windows.
;   Short NB means an IFR without CRC; the CRC preset 0xFF and the
;   inverted CRC byte; the K bit: from memory or search excerpts,
;   unchecked, though consistent with AN1731's 0xC4 residue.

        .imem   512                     ; the macro build: quarters of 128 words
        .thread 0
        .tick   16                      ; the fastest tick proved
        .pins   RX = IN0                ; the bus, for every node
        .equ    SHORT = 32              ; ticks of 2 us
        .equ    LONG = 64
        .equ    SOFL = 100              ; SOF (and EOD)
        .equ    WATCH = 16              ; blind start of every symbol, 32 us
        .equ    EODT = 82               ; passive this long: EOD (> 163 us)
        .equ    EOFT = 120              ; passive this long: EOF (> 239 us)
        .equ    BITT = 34               ; TD - NOW at the edge >= this: short
        .equ    SOFT = 39               ; with TD at EOFT: >= this, no SOF
        .equ    IFS = 150               ; idle before a SOF, 300 us
        .equ    JOIN = IFS - EOFT + 1   ; IFS left >= this: busy, not a SOF
        .equ    TIE = 8                 ; a rise this early still ties
        .equ    MARGIN = 16             ; after our fall the bus falls by then
        .equ    DATA = 0x180            ; node data: DATA + (TID << 5)
        .equ    F_CRC = 1               ; report flags
        .equ    F_SYM = 2
        .equ    F_OVF = 4
        .equ    F_IFR = 8
        .equ    F_SENT = 16
        .equ    F_LOST = 32
        .equ    F_RX = 0x8000

start:  CSRR    r7, TID
        SHRI    r7, 1
        ORI     r7, (1 << 5) | 16       ; one pin: OUT0 (thread 0), OUT1 (2)
        CSRW    OUTGRP, r7
        LDI     r7, 0
        OUT     r7                      ; TX passive
        .csr    BE_CFG, 0x202           ; MSB first, CRC_EN; NRZ, no stuffing
        .csr    BE_PINS, 31             ; SHO's pin write goes nowhere
        .csr    CRC_POLY, 0x1D00        ; x^8+x^4+x^3+x^2+1, left-aligned
        .csr    CRC_INIT, 0xFF00        ; all ones
        CALL    bufp
        POP     r0                      ; 15:8 own address (0: no IFR)
        ST      r0, r2, 0

; Idle: watch the bus for a SOF, the INQ for a frame to send.
idle:   SETD    8
        WAITP   RX, 1, T                ; 16 us of watch
        BNT     rxsof                   ; the bus went active
        SETD    0
        WAITB   INQ_NE, T               ; a test: TD is reached
        BT      idle

; A frame from the host: n bytes into the buffer through the CRC unit,
; then the CRC, inverted.
        .bounded "guarded by the WAITB above; only this thread pops INQ[t]"
        POP     r4                      ; n, header and data bytes
        CALL    bufp
        ADDI    r2, 1
        MOV     r3, r2                  ; the first byte's word
        ANDI    r4, 15
        ADD     r4, r4, r3              ; where the CRC byte goes
        CRCI
        LDI     r6, 0                   ; our frame, no arbitration lost yet
unpack: CMP     r3, r4
        BZ      packed
        POP     r0                      ; a byte in 7:0
        SHLI    r0, 8
        ST      r0, r3, 0
        LDSR    r0
        SHO
        SHO
        SHO
        SHO
        SHO
        SHO
        SHO
        SHO
        ADDI    r3, 1
        JMP     unpack
packed: STCRC   r0
        NOT     r0, r0                  ; sent inverted
        ST      r0, r3, 0               ; 15:8; the low byte is never sent

; Wait for 300 us of idle bus, or join a SOF that follows an EOF.
txwait: WAITP   RX, 0                   ; passive (unbounded)
        SETD    IFS
        WAITP   RX, 1, T
        BT      sof                     ; idle for IFS: our SOF
        CSRR    r2, TD
        CSRR    r7, NOW
        SUB     r2, r2, r7              ; IFS ticks still to go
        CMPI    r2, JOIN
        BC      txwait                  ; idle < 240 us: inside a frame
sof:    LDI     r7, 1
        OUT     r7                      ; our SOF's rise (or with the bus's)
        SETD    0                       ; TD = its tick
        CALL    bufp
        ADDI    r2, 1
        MOV     r3, r2                  ; the first byte
        LDI     r5, 1
        LDI     r2, SOFL
        CALL    txsym
        BNZ     lost

; The bytes from r3 to r4, with arbitration on every symbol.
txnext: LD      r0, r3, 0               ; the byte in 15:8
txbyte: LDI     r1, 8
tbit:   LDI     r7, 0
        SHLI    r0, 1                   ; C = the bit
        BNC     tb0
        LDI     r7, 1
tb0:    XOR     r7, r7, r5              ; 0: the bit equals the level
        LDI     r2, SHORT               ; passive 0, active 1: short
        BZ      tgo
        LDI     r2, LONG
tgo:    CALL    txsym
        BNZ     lost
        DJNZ    r1, tbit
        CMP     r3, r4
        BZ      txdone
        ADDI    r3, 1
        JMP     txnext

; Sent. Our own frame: listen for IFR bytes after it (the receive loop,
; from EOD on). Our IFR answer to another node's frame: report that frame.
txdone: ADD     r7, r6, r6              ; C = F_RX
        BC      rwait                   ; we answered: wait for EOF, report
        ADDI    r3, 1                   ; IFR bytes after our CRC byte
        LDI     r1, 8
        LDI     r0, 0
        JMP     rpas                    ; TD is our last fall

; Arbitration lost: txsym has let go of the bus.
lost:   ADD     r7, r6, r6
        BC      rlost
        MOV16   r7, 0x100               ; one more loss, in 12:8
        ADD     r6, r6, r7
        JMP     txwait                  ; and retry once the bus is idle
rlost:  ORI     r6, F_LOST              ; our IFR lost to another responder
        JMP     rwait

; txsym: one symbol. In: r5 its level, r2 its length in ticks, TD its
; start (the edge that began it). Out, Z = 1: r5 the next level and TD
; the next symbol's start. Out, Z = 0: lost, nothing driven any more.
txsym:  WAITD   WATCH                   ; 32 us into the symbol
        SUBI    r2, WATCH
        CSRR    r7, TD
        ADD     r7, r7, r2
        CSRW    TD, r7                  ; TD = the symbol's end
        CMPI    r5, 0
        BNZ     txact
        JP      RX, 1, txlost           ; active before 32 us: not ours
        LDI     r5, 1
        WAITP   RX, 1, T
        BT      txup                    ; TD: our rise
        CSRR    r2, TD                  ; the bus rose first
        CSRR    r7, NOW
        SUB     r2, r2, r7              ; by this many ticks
        CMPI    r2, TIE
        BC      txlost                  ; a shorter passive symbol: lost
        SETD    0                       ; a tie: the symbol starts now
txup:   OUT     r5                      ; the rise
        XOR     r7, r7, r7              ; Z = 1
        RET
txlost: ORI     r7, 1                   ; Z = 0
        RET
txact:  LDI     r5, 0
        WAITD   0                       ; to TD
        OUT     r5                      ; the fall
        SETD    MARGIN
        WAITP   RX, 0, T
        BT      txlost                  ; still active: a longer symbol, lost
        SETD    0                       ; the next symbol starts at the fall
        XOR     r7, r7, r7
        RET

; Receive. The bus rose at idle: a SOF if it stays active 164 to 239 us.
rxsof:  SETD    0                       ; the rise, within a slot or two
        MOV16   r6, F_RX
        WAITD   WATCH
        JP      RX, 1, rxs1
        JMP     idle                    ; under 32 us: noise, ignored
rxs1:   SETD    EOFT - WATCH
        WAITP   RX, 0, T
        BT      rsym                    ; 240 us or more: a break
        CSRR    r2, TD                  ; the edge: r2 = TD - NOW, the ticks
        CSRR    r7, NOW                 ; short of the timeout; TD = NOW,
        SUB     r2, r2, r7
        SETD    0                       ; the next symbol's start
        CMPI    r2, SOFT
        BC      rsym                    ; 163 us or less: not a SOF
        CALL    bufp
        ADDI    r2, 1
        MOV     r3, r2                  ; the first byte's word
        LDI     r1, 8
        LDI     r0, 0
        CRCI

; A passive symbol from TD on: a 0 if short, a 1 if long, else EOD.
rpas:   WAITD   WATCH
        JP      RX, 1, rsym             ; under 34 us: invalid
        SETD    EODT - WATCH            ; TD = start + 82 ticks
        WAITP   RX, 1, T
        BT      reod
        CSRR    r2, TD                  ; the edge: r2 = TD - NOW, the ticks
        CSRR    r7, NOW                 ; short of the timeout; TD = NOW,
        SUB     r2, r2, r7
        SETD    0                       ; the next symbol's start
        LDI     r7, 1
        CMPI    r2, BITT
        BNC     rp1                     ; long: a 1
        LDI     r7, 0
rp1:    CALL    rbit
; An active symbol from TD on: a 1 if short, a 0 if long.
ract:   WAITD   WATCH
        JP      RX, 0, rsym
        SETD    EODT - WATCH
        WAITP   RX, 0, T
        BT      rsym                    ; over 163 us inside a frame
        CSRR    r2, TD                  ; the edge: r2 = TD - NOW, the ticks
        CSRR    r7, NOW                 ; short of the timeout; TD = NOW,
        SUB     r2, r2, r7
        SETD    0                       ; the next symbol's start
        LDI     r7, 0
        CMPI    r2, BITT
        BNC     ra1                     ; long: a 0
        LDI     r7, 1
ra1:    CALL    rbit
        JMP     rpas

; A symbol error: wait for EOF and report what was received.
rsym:   ORI     r6, F_SYM
rwait:  WAITP   RX, 0
        SETD    EOFT
        WAITP   RX, 1, T
        BNT     rwait
        JMP     report

; rbit: the bit in r7 into r0; a whole byte to the buffer (at r3) and
; through the CRC unit (eight SHO to no pin, unrolled).
rbit:   ADD     r0, r0, r0
        OR      r0, r0, r7
        DJNZ    r1, rbr
        LDI     r1, 8
        SHLI    r0, 8                   ; the byte in 15:8
        ST      r0, r3, 0
        LDSR    r0
        SHO
        SHO
        SHO
        SHO
        SHO
        SHO
        SHO
        SHO
        LDI     r0, 0
        ADDI    r3, 1
        MOV     r7, r3
        ANDI    r7, 31
        BNZ     rbr
        SUBI    r3, 1                   ; full: keep the last word
        ORI     r6, F_OVF
rbr:    RET

; EOD: 164 us passive. The first one ends the frame proper: for a received
; frame its byte count into 12:8, the CRC check, perhaps our IFR. Then an
; NB may start an IFR, or 240 us of passive bus is EOF.
reod:   CMPI    r1, 8
        BZ      re1
        ORI     r6, F_SYM               ; not on a byte boundary
re1:    MOV     r7, r6
        ANDI    r7, F_IFR
        BNZ     rnb                     ; the EOD after an IFR
        ADD     r7, r6, r6
        BNC     rnb                     ; our own frame: nothing to check
        CALL    bufp                    ; r2 = the configuration word
        SUB     r0, r3, r2
        SUBI    r0, 1                   ; the frame's bytes, CRC included
        SHLI    r0, 8
        OR      r6, r6, r0
        STCRC   r7
        MOV16   r0, 0xC400              ; the residue of a good frame
        CMP     r7, r0
        BZ      crcok
        ORI     r6, F_CRC
        JMP     rnb
crcok:  LD      r0, r2, 0               ; own address in 15:8, 0: no IFR
        CMPI    r0, 0
        BZ      rnb
        LD      r7, r2, 2               ; the target byte, the second
        XOR     r7, r7, r0
        BNZ     rnb                     ; not addressed to us
        LD      r7, r2, 1               ; the first header byte
        SHRI    r7, 11
        ANDI    r7, 1
        BNZ     rnb                     ; K = 1: no IFR wanted
; Our IFR: the NB 200 us after the last fall, then our address byte (r0).
        ORI     r6, F_SENT
        LDI     r5, 1
        WAITD   SOFL - EODT             ; TD was start + 82: to start + 100
        OUT     r5                      ; the NB's rise
        MOV     r4, r3                  ; one byte: txbyte ends at txdone
        LDI     r2, SHORT               ; NB short: an IFR without CRC
        CALL    txsym
        BNZ     rlj
        JMP     txbyte
rlj:    JMP     lost

        .org    start + 0x101           ; over thread 2's reset vector
rnb:    SETD    EOFT - EODT             ; to EOF
        WAITP   RX, 1, T
        BT      report                  ; EOF: the frame is over
        CSRR    r2, TD                  ; the edge: r2 = TD - NOW, the ticks
        CSRR    r7, NOW                 ; short of the timeout; TD = NOW,
        SUB     r2, r2, r7
        SETD    0                       ; the next symbol's start
        MOV     r7, r6
        ANDI    r7, F_IFR
        BNZ     rsym                    ; a second NB
        ORI     r6, F_IFR
        LDI     r1, 9                   ; the NB enters r0 as a ninth bit
        JMP     ract                    ; and leaves it with the SHLI 8

; The report: the status word, the byte count, then the bytes, one a word
; in 7:0: a received frame's from the first byte, the IFR after our own
; frame from the word after its CRC byte (r4).
report: CALL    bufp
        ADDI    r2, 1
        ADD     r7, r6, r6
        BC      rp0
        MOV     r2, r4
        ADDI    r2, 1
rp0:    PUSH    r6
        SUB     r7, r3, r2
        PUSH    r7
rpk:    CMP     r2, r3
        BNZ     rpk1
        JMP     idle
rpk1:   LD      r0, r2, 0
        SHRI    r0, 8
        PUSH    r0
        ADDI    r2, 1
        JMP     rpk

; bufp: r2 = this node's data (the configuration word, then 31 bytes):
; DATA + 32 * TID, so 0x180 for thread 0, 0x1C0 for thread 2.
bufp:   CSRR    r2, TID
        SHLI    r2, 5
        MOV16   r7, DATA
        ADD     r2, r2, r7
        RET

        .thread 2                       ; a second node: the same code
        JMP     start
